Remember the Core Principles of Sports Reporting
• Accuracy and Timeliness: Lead with the most important facts—such as final scores, key turning points, or major roster updates—in the first or second paragraph. Avoid burying crucial outcomes.
• Structure by Format:
	• Game Reports: Use the inverted pyramid or chronological storytelling to highlight scoring plays, stats, and tactical context.
	• Hard News (e.g., injuries, firings, transfers): Treat with the same verification standards as traditional hard news, avoiding rumors or unverified social media chatter.
	• Feature Stories & Profiles: Focus on background, human-interest angles, and deeper narratives beyond the basic box score.
• Sourcing and Verification:
	• Minimize the use of anonymous sources and vet claims thoroughly with editors.
	• Cross-check team press releases, coach interviews, and player statements against independent observations and stats.
• Ethical Standards & Neutrality:
	• Maintain editorial independence from teams and leagues.
	• Do not accept improper gifts, paid sponsorships, or engage in betting on the sports or leagues you cover.
	• Separate analytical opinion/columns from straight-ahead factual reporting.
• Use of Quotes and Stats: Support narrative tension and emotional context with direct quotes from participants, paired with accurate numerical data (box scores, advanced metrics).

